export const seedSuggestions: SeedSuggestion[] = [
  { category: 'e-commerce', title: 'Product Listing', content: 'The product listing screen shows a grid or list of purchasable items, usually with filters, sorting, and category navigation. It typically follows the homepage or a category/search entry point and leads into the Product Detail screen when an item is tapped. It is expected in any storefront because customers need a way to browse and narrow down inventory before committing to a single item. Also called catalog view, product grid, or browse/shop page.' },
    { category: 'e-commerce', title: "Product Detail", content: "The product detail screen presents full information about a single item: images, price, variants (size/color), description, reviews, and an add-to-cart action. It sits between Product Listing and Cart in the flow, reached by tapping an item and exited by adding to cart or navigating back. It is expected because shoppers need enough information to decide before purchasing; also called PDP, item page, or product page." },
    { category: 'e-commerce', title: "Cart", content: "The cart (or bag/basket) screen lists items the user has selected to purchase, with quantity editing, price subtotal, and removal options. It follows Product Detail (after add-to-cart) and precedes the Shipping Address or checkout step. It is a near-universal checkpoint in commerce flows because it lets users review and adjust their order before committing to buy." },
    { category: 'e-commerce', title: "Shipping Address", content: "The shipping address step collects where the customer's order should be delivered. It typically appears after the Cart or Bag screen and immediately before the Payment step in a checkout flow. Also called delivery details or address entry, it is expected in any physical-goods purchase flow and often includes saved-address selection and address validation." },
    { category: 'e-commerce', title: "Payment", content: "The payment screen collects card, wallet, or other payment method details and confirms the amount to be charged. It follows Shipping Address and precedes Order Confirmation, and is the mandatory final step before an order is placed. Also called checkout payment, billing details, or payment method selection, it is expected in every transactional flow that involves money changing hands." },
    { category: 'e-commerce', title: "Order Confirmation", content: "The order confirmation screen reassures the buyer that their purchase succeeded, showing an order number, summary, and estimated delivery date. It appears immediately after Payment is submitted and often links forward to Order Tracking. It is expected because users need explicit proof of a successful transaction; also called thank-you page, receipt screen, or purchase success." },
    { category: 'e-commerce', title: "Order Tracking", content: "The order tracking screen shows the current fulfillment status of a placed order, such as processing, shipped, or delivered, often with a courier tracking link. It is reached from an order history list or the confirmation email/screen, after Order Confirmation. It is expected because post-purchase visibility reduces customer anxiety and support requests; also called shipment tracking or delivery status." },
    { category: 'e-commerce', title: "Returns", content: "The returns screen lets a customer initiate a return or exchange for a delivered order, selecting items, a reason, and a refund or replacement method. It is accessed from Order Tracking or order history, well after Order Confirmation, and typically leads to a refund status screen. It is expected in e-commerce because buyer's remorse and defective items are routine; also called return/exchange request or RMA flow." },

    { category: "marketplace", title: "Browse Listings", content: "The browse listings screen shows a searchable, filterable feed of items or services posted by different sellers, often with location and price filters. It is usually the marketplace's home or search entry point and leads into a Listing Detail screen. It is expected because marketplaces aggregate many independent sellers and users need discovery tools; also called listings feed, search results, or marketplace home." },
    { category: "marketplace", title: "Listing Detail", content: "The listing detail screen shows full information about a single item or service posted by a seller, including photos, price, condition, and description. It follows Browse Listings and typically leads to Seller Profile, Chat/Messaging, or Make Offer. It is expected because buyers need enough detail to evaluate a peer-to-peer offering before contacting the seller; also called item page or ad detail." },
    { category: "marketplace", title: "Seller Profile", content: "The seller profile screen shows information about the person or business offering an item, including their rating, reviews, and other active listings. It is usually reached from a Listing Detail page and can lead to Chat/Messaging or a new Browse Listings filtered by that seller. It is expected in marketplaces because trust between strangers is central to peer-to-peer transactions; also called vendor profile or storefront." },
    { category: "marketplace", title: "Chat/Messaging", content: "The chat or messaging screen lets a buyer and seller communicate directly about a listing, negotiate details, or arrange logistics. It typically opens from a Listing Detail or Seller Profile screen and can lead into Make Offer or Checkout. It is expected because marketplace transactions often need clarification before purchase; also called inbox, direct message thread, or inquiry chat." },
    { category: "marketplace", title: "Make Offer", content: "The make offer screen lets a buyer propose a price different from the listed price, which the seller can accept, reject, or counter. It typically follows Listing Detail or Chat/Messaging and, once accepted, leads into Checkout. It is expected in marketplaces where prices are negotiable, distinguishing them from fixed-price retail; also called bid, counteroffer, or negotiate price." },
    { category: "marketplace", title: "Checkout", content: "The checkout screen finalizes a marketplace purchase, collecting payment and shipping or pickup details once a price is agreed. It follows Make Offer or a direct buy action and leads to a confirmation screen. It is expected as the transactional close of any marketplace flow; also called complete purchase or pay for item." },
    { category: "marketplace", title: "Review & Rating", content: "The review and rating screen lets a buyer or seller rate the other party and leave feedback after a transaction completes. It appears after checkout and delivery/handoff are complete, and results feed back into the counterparty's Seller Profile. It is expected because reputation systems are what let strangers trust each other on marketplaces; also called feedback, leave a review, or rate this transaction." },
    { category: "marketplace", title: "Dispute Resolution", content: "The dispute resolution screen lets a buyer or seller report a problem with a transaction, such as a non-delivered or misrepresented item, and request marketplace mediation. It is reached from order/transaction history after Checkout, separate from the normal review path. It is expected because peer-to-peer trust occasionally breaks down and platforms need a formal escalation path; also called report a problem, open a case, or claims center." },

    { category: "saas-dashboard", title: "Onboarding Walkthrough", content: "The onboarding walkthrough guides a new user through initial setup, such as connecting data sources, inviting teammates, or configuring preferences. It appears immediately after Sign Up and before the user reaches the Main Dashboard for the first time. It is expected because SaaS products are often complex enough that users need guided setup to reach value quickly; also called setup wizard, getting started flow, or first-run experience." },
    { category: "saas-dashboard", title: "Main Dashboard", content: "The main dashboard is the primary landing screen after login, summarizing key metrics, recent activity, and shortcuts to core features. It follows Sign In or Onboarding Walkthrough and is the hub from which users navigate to Settings, Billing, or feature-specific screens. It is expected as the central home base of any SaaS product; also called home screen, overview, or control panel." },
    { category: "saas-dashboard", title: "Settings", content: "The settings screen lets a user configure account, workspace, or application preferences such as profile info, integrations, and notification rules. It is reached from the Main Dashboard's navigation and typically has no fixed next screen, acting as a branch point. It is expected in every SaaS product because users need control over how the tool behaves; also called preferences, account settings, or configuration." },
    { category: "saas-dashboard", title: "Billing/Subscription", content: "The billing screen shows the user's current plan, payment method, invoices, and upgrade/downgrade options. It is usually reached from Settings or a dashboard banner, and leads to a Payment step when changing plans. It is expected in any paid SaaS product because customers need self-serve visibility into what they're being charged; also called plan management, subscription settings, or invoices." },
    { category: "saas-dashboard", title: "Team/Member Management", content: "The team management screen lets an admin invite, remove, or change the roles of other users within the same workspace or account. It is reached from Settings or the Main Dashboard and leads to an invite confirmation or empty pending-invite state. It is expected in multi-user SaaS tools because collaboration requires access control; also called members, user management, or workspace roles." },
    { category: "saas-dashboard", title: "Notifications Center", content: "The notifications center lists system alerts, mentions, and activity updates relevant to the user, often with read/unread states. It is typically accessible from a bell icon on the Main Dashboard at any point in the flow. It is expected because SaaS products generate ongoing background activity users need to stay aware of; also called activity feed, alerts, or inbox." },
    { category: "saas-dashboard", title: "Empty State", content: "The empty state screen appears when a user has no data yet in a given section, such as no projects or no reports, and typically shows guidance or a call-to-action to create the first item. It appears in place of the Main Dashboard or a feature screen before any content exists, and leads into a creation flow. It is expected because new accounts and new sections always start with nothing in them; also called zero state or no-data placeholder." },
    { category: "saas-dashboard", title: "Usage Analytics", content: "The usage analytics screen shows charts and metrics about how the product or account is being used over time, such as active users or API calls. It is reached from the Main Dashboard or Settings and has no fixed next screen. It is expected in SaaS products, especially usage-based ones, because customers and admins need to monitor consumption and value; also called analytics dashboard, insights, or reports." },

    { category: "social-network", title: "News Feed", content: "The news feed is the scrollable stream of posts from people or pages a user follows, usually the default screen after login. It follows Sign In and leads into Post Detail, Comments, or Profile Page when a user taps an item. It is expected as the core discovery surface of any social product; also called timeline, home feed, or activity stream." },
    { category: "social-network", title: "Profile Page", content: "The profile page shows a single user's posts, bio, photo, and follower/following counts. It is reached by tapping a name or avatar anywhere in the app, most often from the News Feed, and can lead to Direct Messages or a Follow Request. It is expected because social products are organized around individual identities; also called user profile or bio page." },
    { category: "social-network", title: "Post Composer", content: "The post composer is the screen or overlay where a user writes and publishes new content, attaching text, images, or video. It is opened from the News Feed via a compose button and, once submitted, returns the user to the feed with the new post visible. It is expected because content creation is the core input action of any social platform; also called create post, new post, or compose screen." },
    { category: "social-network", title: "Comments/Replies", content: "The comments screen shows the thread of replies underneath a specific post, along with an input to add a new comment. It is reached by tapping a post's comment icon from the News Feed or Profile Page. It is expected because reciprocal conversation around content is central to social engagement; also called replies, discussion thread, or post comments." },
    { category: "social-network", title: "Direct Messages", content: "The direct messages screen lists private one-to-one or group conversations and opens into individual chat threads. It is typically reached from a dedicated navigation tab or from a Profile Page's message button. It is expected because most social networks pair public feeds with private communication; also called DMs, inbox, or private chat." },
    { category: "social-network", title: "Notifications", content: "The notifications screen lists likes, comments, follows, and mentions relevant to the user, each linking back to the relevant post or profile. It is reached from a bell icon accessible throughout the app. It is expected because users need to know when others interact with their content; also called activity, alerts, or notification feed." },
    { category: "social-network", title: "Friend/Follow Request", content: "The follow request screen lets a user send, accept, or decline connection requests, depending on whether the platform uses mutual friending or one-way following. It is reached from a Profile Page's follow button or from a pending-requests list in Notifications. It is expected because managing the user's social graph is a core account action; also called connection request or follow/unfollow." },
    { category: "social-network", title: "Search/Discover", content: "The search and discover screen lets users find other people, hashtags, or trending content outside their existing feed. It is reached from a search icon accessible from the News Feed and leads into Profile Page or post detail views. It is expected because social platforms need a way for users to expand their network beyond who they already follow; also called explore, search, or discover tab." },

    { category: "media-streaming", title: "Home/Browse", content: "The home/browse screen shows curated rows of titles such as trending, recommended, and genre-based carousels. It is the landing screen after Sign In or Profile Selection and leads into a Content Detail screen when a title is selected. It is expected because streaming platforms have large catalogs that need curated entry points; also called browse, discover, or catalog home." },
    { category: "media-streaming", title: "Content Detail", content: "The content detail screen shows information about a single movie, show, or episode: synopsis, cast, ratings, and related titles. It follows Home/Browse or Search and leads into the Video Player when the user presses play. It is expected because viewers need context before committing time to watch something; also called title page, show detail, or movie info." },
    { category: "media-streaming", title: "Video Player", content: "The video player screen plays the selected content with playback controls, subtitle options, and quality settings. It follows Content Detail and, on completion, may lead to an autoplay-next screen or back to Home/Browse. It is expected as the terminal, value-delivering screen of any streaming flow; also called playback screen or watch screen." },
    { category: "media-streaming", title: "Search", content: "The search screen lets users find specific titles, actors, or genres by typing a query, returning a filtered results list. It is reached from a search icon on Home/Browse and leads into Content Detail. It is expected because large catalogs are hard to browse without direct lookup; also called find content or catalog search." },
    { category: "media-streaming", title: "Watchlist/My List", content: "The watchlist screen shows titles a user has saved to watch later, added from Content Detail via a plus or bookmark action. It is reached from a dedicated navigation tab and leads back into Content Detail. It is expected because streaming sessions are often split from discovery sessions; also called my list, saved, or favorites." },
    { category: "media-streaming", title: "Subscription Plans", content: "The subscription plans screen shows available pricing tiers, such as ad-supported or premium, and lets the user choose or change one. It typically appears during Sign Up or from account Settings and leads into a Payment step. It is expected because most streaming services are subscription-funded and need a plan-selection step; also called plan selection or membership tiers." },
    { category: "media-streaming", title: "Continue Watching", content: "The continue watching row or screen shows in-progress titles with resume points, letting users pick up where they left off. It usually appears near the top of Home/Browse and leads directly into the Video Player at the saved timestamp. It is expected because streaming sessions are frequently interrupted across devices; also called resume watching or up next." },
    { category: "media-streaming", title: "Profile Selection", content: "The profile selection screen lets a household choose which member profile, such as a specific person or kids' profile, is using the app. It typically appears right after Sign In and before Home/Browse. It is expected in streaming platforms because personalized recommendations and parental controls depend on distinguishing household members; also called who's watching or switch profile." },

    { category: "booking-reservation", title: "Search/Availability", content: "The search/availability screen lets a user specify criteria such as date, time, party size, or location and returns matching available slots or venues. It is typically the entry point of a booking flow and leads into a Listing/Item Detail screen. It is expected because availability constantly changes and users need to filter for what's actually bookable; also called check availability or find a time." },
    { category: "booking-reservation", title: "Listing/Item Detail", content: "The listing detail screen shows full information about the specific venue, room, table, or service being booked, including photos, amenities, and pricing. It follows Search/Availability and leads into Date & Time Selection. It is expected because users need to confirm a specific option meets their needs before committing to a date; also called venue detail, property page, or item overview." },
    { category: "booking-reservation", title: "Date & Time Selection", content: "The date and time selection screen lets the user pick a specific slot from the available options for the chosen listing. It follows Listing/Item Detail and precedes Guest/Attendee Details. It is expected because bookings are fundamentally time-bound commitments; also called pick a slot, choose date, or select time." },
    { category: "booking-reservation", title: "Guest/Attendee Details", content: "This screen collects the names, contact information, and any special requests for the people included in the booking. It follows Date & Time Selection and precedes Payment. It is expected because the business needs to know who is actually arriving, separate from who is paying; also called party details, attendee info, or guest information." },
    { category: "booking-reservation", title: "Payment", content: "The payment screen collects payment or deposit details to secure the reservation. It follows Guest/Attendee Details and precedes Booking Confirmation. It is expected in most booking flows to reduce no-shows and formalize the commitment; also called checkout, secure booking, or pay deposit." },
    { category: "booking-reservation", title: "Booking Confirmation", content: "The booking confirmation screen shows a summary of the completed reservation, including date, time, location, and a reference number. It appears immediately after Payment and often links to My Bookings. It is expected because users need proof and details of what they just reserved; also called reservation confirmed or booking summary." },
    { category: "booking-reservation", title: "My Bookings", content: "The my bookings screen lists a user's upcoming and past reservations, each linking to more detail or a cancellation option. It is reached from account navigation, independent of any single booking flow, well after Booking Confirmation. It is expected because users need an ongoing place to manage multiple reservations over time; also called reservations, my trips, or upcoming bookings." },
    { category: "booking-reservation", title: "Cancellation/Refund", content: "The cancellation screen lets a user cancel or modify an existing reservation and shows the applicable refund or penalty terms. It is reached from My Bookings, well after the original Booking Confirmation. It is expected because plans change and cancellation policies must be clearly presented; also called cancel booking, modify reservation, or refund request." },

    { category: "fintech-banking", title: "Account Overview", content: "The account overview screen shows balances across a user's accounts, such as checking or savings, along with recent transactions at a glance. It is the landing screen after Sign In and branches into Transaction History, Money Transfer, and Card Management. It is expected as the financial hub of any banking product; also called dashboard, accounts summary, or home." },
    { category: "fintech-banking", title: "Transaction History", content: "The transaction history screen lists past debits and credits with dates, amounts, and merchant or counterparty names, often filterable by date or category. It is reached from Account Overview and typically leads to a single transaction detail view. It is expected because users need to audit and understand their financial activity; also called activity, statement view, or transactions list." },
    { category: "fintech-banking", title: "Money Transfer", content: "The money transfer screen lets a user send funds to another account, whether internally, to another bank, or to a person via a linked identifier. It is reached from Account Overview and leads to a confirmation step, often requiring authentication. It is expected as one of the core actions of any banking app; also called send money, transfer funds, or pay someone." },
    { category: "fintech-banking", title: "Bill Payment", content: "The bill payment screen lets a user pay a recurring or one-time bill to a registered biller, such as a utility or credit card company. It is reached from Account Overview and leads to a payment confirmation. It is expected because bill pay is a primary reason users adopt banking apps; also called pay bills, billers, or scheduled payments." },
    { category: "fintech-banking", title: "Card Management", content: "The card management screen lets a user view, freeze, or set limits on a debit or credit card linked to their account. It is reached from Account Overview or Settings and has no fixed next screen. It is expected because card security controls are a standard self-service feature in modern banking apps; also called manage cards, card controls, or card settings." },
    { category: "fintech-banking", title: "Statements/Documents", content: "The statements screen lets a user view or download monthly account statements and tax documents. It is reached from Account Overview or Settings and typically opens a PDF viewer or download action. It is expected because statements are often required for record-keeping, loans, or tax filing; also called documents, monthly statements, or e-statements." },
    { category: "fintech-banking", title: "Budgeting/Insights", content: "The budgeting screen categorizes spending and shows trends, budgets, or savings goals based on transaction data. It is reached from Account Overview and draws on data from Transaction History. It is expected in modern fintech apps because users increasingly want proactive financial guidance, not just record-keeping; also called insights, spending analysis, or budget tracker." },
    { category: "fintech-banking", title: "Support/Dispute", content: "The support screen lets a user report a fraudulent charge, dispute a transaction, or contact customer service. It is reached from Transaction History or Account Overview when something looks wrong. It is expected because financial errors and fraud require a clear escalation path; also called report fraud, dispute a charge, or help center." },

    { category: "e-learning", title: "Course Catalog", content: "The course catalog screen lists available courses with filters for topic, level, or price. It is typically the landing screen after Sign In and leads into a Course Detail screen when a course is selected. It is expected because learning platforms host many courses that users need to browse and compare; also called browse courses, catalog, or explore." },
    { category: "e-learning", title: "Course Detail", content: "The course detail screen shows a syllabus, instructor info, reviews, and pricing for a single course. It follows Course Catalog and leads into Enrollment/Payment or directly into the first lesson if already enrolled. It is expected because learners need to evaluate fit and quality before committing time and money; also called course overview or syllabus page." },
    { category: "e-learning", title: "Lesson/Video Player", content: "The lesson player screen plays a video or displays lesson content, with navigation to the next lesson and a progress indicator. It follows Enrollment/Payment or Course Detail and precedes Quiz/Assessment. It is expected as the core content-delivery screen of any e-learning flow; also called lecture view, video lesson, or module player." },
    { category: "e-learning", title: "Quiz/Assessment", content: "The quiz screen presents questions to test comprehension of preceding lesson content and shows a score on submission. It follows a Lesson/Video Player and its result feeds into the Progress Dashboard. It is expected because assessment is how e-learning platforms verify and reinforce learning; also called test, exam, or knowledge check." },
    { category: "e-learning", title: "Progress Dashboard", content: "The progress dashboard shows a learner's completion percentage across lessons and courses, along with streaks or scores. It is reached from account navigation and aggregates data from completed Lesson and Quiz screens. It is expected because visible progress motivates continued learning; also called my learning, progress tracker, or dashboard." },
    { category: "e-learning", title: "Certificate", content: "The certificate screen shows or generates a completion certificate once a learner finishes all required lessons and assessments in a course. It is reached from the Progress Dashboard after 100% completion. It is expected because many learners take courses specifically for credential proof; also called completion certificate or diploma." },
    { category: "e-learning", title: "Discussion Forum", content: "The discussion forum screen lets learners post questions and answers related to a specific course or lesson. It is typically reached from Course Detail or a Lesson screen and threads persist independent of the linear lesson flow. It is expected because peer and instructor interaction improves learning outcomes; also called Q&A, course discussion, or community." },
    { category: "e-learning", title: "Enrollment/Payment", content: "The enrollment screen collects payment (or confirms free enrollment) to grant a learner access to a course's content. It follows Course Detail and precedes the first Lesson/Video Player screen. It is expected as the transactional gate between browsing and consuming paid course content; also called enroll now, buy course, or checkout." },

    { category: "healthcare-telehealth", title: "Symptom/Reason Intake", content: "The intake screen asks the patient to describe their symptoms or reason for the visit before matching them with a provider. It typically appears right after Sign In and precedes Provider Search. It is expected because triage information helps route patients to the right type of care; also called reason for visit, symptom checker, or intake form." },
    { category: "healthcare-telehealth", title: "Provider Search", content: "The provider search screen lists available doctors or specialists, filterable by specialty, availability, or insurance accepted. It follows Symptom/Reason Intake and leads into Appointment Scheduling. It is expected because patients need to choose who they see, not just when; also called find a doctor, browse providers, or specialist search." },
    { category: "healthcare-telehealth", title: "Appointment Scheduling", content: "The appointment scheduling screen lets a patient pick an available time slot with the chosen provider. It follows Provider Search and precedes the Video Consultation at the scheduled time. It is expected because telehealth visits, like in-person ones, are time-bound appointments; also called book appointment, choose a time, or schedule visit." },
    { category: "healthcare-telehealth", title: "Video Consultation", content: "The video consultation screen hosts the live video call between patient and provider, often with a waiting room state beforehand. It follows Appointment Scheduling at the scheduled time and precedes a post-visit summary or Prescription screen. It is expected as the core value-delivering screen of a telehealth flow; also called virtual visit, video call, or consultation room." },
    { category: "healthcare-telehealth", title: "Prescription/E-Prescription", content: "The prescription screen shows medications the provider has prescribed during or after the visit, often with a send-to-pharmacy action. It follows the Video Consultation and can lead to Medical Records. It is expected because prescribing medication is a common outcome of a healthcare visit; also called e-prescribing, medication orders, or prescription summary." },
    { category: "healthcare-telehealth", title: "Medical Records", content: "The medical records screen shows a patient's visit history, diagnoses, lab results, and prescriptions over time. It is reached from account navigation, aggregating data from past Video Consultation and Prescription screens. It is expected because continuity of care depends on accessible history; also called health records, visit history, or chart." },
    { category: "healthcare-telehealth", title: "Billing/Insurance", content: "The billing screen shows the cost of a visit, insurance coverage applied, and any co-pay due, with a payment action. It typically appears during Appointment Scheduling or after the Video Consultation. It is expected because healthcare costs and insurance processing are central concerns for patients; also called insurance details, copay, or visit cost." },
    { category: "healthcare-telehealth", title: "Follow-up Reminder", content: "The follow-up reminder screen or notification prompts a patient to schedule a subsequent visit or take a recurring action like refilling medication. It is triggered some time after a Video Consultation or Prescription screen. It is expected because ongoing care often requires more than a single visit; also called follow-up care, reminder, or next steps." },

    { category: "food-delivery", title: "Restaurant Listing", content: "The restaurant listing screen shows nearby restaurants with filters for cuisine, rating, and delivery time. It is typically the home screen after Sign In and leads into a Menu/Restaurant Detail screen. It is expected because delivery apps aggregate many restaurants that users need to browse by proximity; also called restaurant search, browse restaurants, or home feed." },
    { category: "food-delivery", title: "Menu/Restaurant Detail", content: "The menu screen shows a restaurant's dishes with prices, descriptions, and photos, letting the user add items to a cart. It follows Restaurant Listing and precedes Cart. It is expected because users need to select specific items before ordering; also called restaurant page or food menu." },
    { category: "food-delivery", title: "Cart", content: "The cart screen lists selected food items with quantities and a running total before checkout. It follows Menu/Restaurant Detail and precedes Delivery Address. It is expected as the review step before committing to an order, consistent across delivery and retail commerce; also called bag, order summary, or basket." },
    { category: "food-delivery", title: "Delivery Address", content: "The delivery address screen confirms or selects where the food order should be delivered. It follows Cart and precedes Payment. It is expected because delivery logistics and restaurant matching depend on a confirmed address; also called delivery location or drop-off address." },
    { category: "food-delivery", title: "Order Tracking", content: "The order tracking screen shows real-time status of a food order, such as preparing, out for delivery, or a live courier map. It follows Payment/order placement and precedes a delivered confirmation. It is expected because food delivery has a short, time-sensitive fulfillment window users want visibility into; also called live tracking, delivery status, or track order." },
    { category: "food-delivery", title: "Payment", content: "The payment screen collects payment method and confirms the order total including delivery fees and tip. It follows Delivery Address and precedes Order Tracking. It is expected as the transactional close of the ordering flow; also called checkout or place order." },
    { category: "food-delivery", title: "Rating/Review", content: "The rating screen lets a user rate the food, restaurant, and delivery experience after an order is completed. It appears after Order Tracking shows delivery complete. It is expected because delivery quality varies by courier and restaurant, and ratings inform future users; also called rate your order or leave feedback." },
    { category: "food-delivery", title: "Order History", content: "The order history screen lists a user's past orders with the option to reorder the same items. It is reached from account navigation, independent of any single order's flow. It is expected because repeat ordering from favorite restaurants is common behavior; also called past orders, reorder, or order history." },

    { category: "travel", title: "Search Flights/Hotels", content: "The search screen lets a user specify origin, destination, dates, and traveler count to find available flights, hotels, or other travel options. It is the entry point of a travel booking flow and leads into a Results Listing screen. It is expected because travel inventory is vast and time/date dependent; also called flight search, hotel search, or trip search." },
    { category: "travel", title: "Results Listing", content: "The results listing screen shows matching flights or hotels with prices, filters, and sort options. It follows Search Flights/Hotels and leads into an Item Detail screen. It is expected because users need to compare multiple options before choosing; also called search results, flight results, or available options." },
    { category: "travel", title: "Item Detail", content: "The item detail screen shows full details of a specific flight or hotel, including itinerary, amenities, and cancellation policy. It follows Results Listing and precedes Passenger/Guest Details. It is expected because travel purchases are high-stakes enough to require full detail review before booking; also called flight details, hotel detail, or fare details." },
    { category: "travel", title: "Passenger/Guest Details", content: "This screen collects the names and travel document or contact information for each passenger or guest included in the booking. It follows Item Detail and precedes Payment. It is expected because travel bookings are tied to specific named individuals for check-in and legal purposes; also called traveler info or guest information." },
    { category: "travel", title: "Payment", content: "The payment screen collects payment details to finalize a flight or hotel booking. It follows Passenger/Guest Details and precedes Itinerary/Confirmation. It is expected as the transactional close of any travel booking flow; also called checkout or book now." },
    { category: "travel", title: "Itinerary/Confirmation", content: "The itinerary confirmation screen summarizes the booked trip with dates, confirmation numbers, and downloadable tickets or vouchers. It appears immediately after Payment and links to Trip Management. It is expected because travelers need proof of booking and a reference for later; also called booking confirmation, e-ticket, or trip summary." },
    { category: "travel", title: "Trip Management", content: "The trip management screen lists a user's upcoming and past trips, each linking to itinerary details, changes, or cancellation. It is reached from account navigation, well after Itinerary/Confirmation. It is expected because travelers often manage multiple bookings, add-ons, or changes over time; also called my trips, bookings, or travel dashboard." },
    { category: "travel", title: "Check-in", content: "The check-in screen lets a traveler check in online for a flight or hotel ahead of arrival, often issuing a boarding pass or digital key. It is reached from Trip Management shortly before the travel date. It is expected because online check-in is now a standard step that shortens in-person processing; also called online check-in or boarding pass." },

    { category: "real-estate", title: "Property Search", content: "The property search screen lets a user filter listings by location, price, bedrooms, and property type. It is typically the home or landing screen and leads into a Property Listing Detail screen. It is expected because real estate inventory needs heavy filtering to be useful; also called browse properties, search homes, or listings search." },
    { category: "real-estate", title: "Property Listing Detail", content: "The property listing detail screen shows full information about a single property: photos, price, floor plan, and description. It follows Property Search or Map View and leads into Schedule Tour/Viewing or Contact Agent. It is expected because buyers or renters need substantial detail before pursuing a property further; also called listing page or property detail." },
    { category: "real-estate", title: "Map View", content: "The map view screen shows property listings pinned on a geographic map, letting users browse spatially rather than by list. It is an alternate mode reachable from Property Search and leads into Property Listing Detail when a pin is tapped. It is expected because location is often the primary decision factor in real estate; also called map search or nearby listings." },
    { category: "real-estate", title: "Schedule Tour/Viewing", content: "The schedule tour screen lets a user request or book an in-person or virtual viewing of a property. It follows Property Listing Detail and typically leads to a confirmation of the scheduled time. It is expected because real estate decisions usually require seeing the property before committing; also called book a viewing, request a tour, or schedule showing." },
    { category: "real-estate", title: "Contact Agent", content: "The contact agent screen lets a user send a message or inquiry to the listing agent about a specific property. It follows Property Listing Detail and opens into a message thread or callback request. It is expected because most real estate transactions still involve human negotiation and guidance; also called inquire, contact seller, or ask a question." },
    { category: "real-estate", title: "Saved/Favorites", content: "The saved listings screen shows properties a user has bookmarked from Property Listing Detail or search results for later comparison. It is reached from account navigation and leads back into Property Listing Detail. It is expected because property searches are typically long, multi-session processes; also called favorites, saved homes, or watchlist." },
    { category: "real-estate", title: "Mortgage Calculator", content: "The mortgage calculator screen lets a user estimate monthly payments based on price, down payment, and interest rate. It is typically reached from Property Listing Detail as a supporting tool. It is expected because affordability is a central concern in property decisions; also called payment calculator or affordability estimator." },
    { category: "real-estate", title: "Application/Offer Submission", content: "This screen lets a user submit a rental application or purchase offer for a specific property, including documents and terms. It follows Schedule Tour/Viewing or Contact Agent, once the user decides to proceed. It is expected because formalizing interest into a documented offer is the conversion point of a real estate flow; also called submit offer, apply now, or rental application." },

    { category: "job-board", title: "Job Search", content: "The job search screen lets a user filter open roles by title, location, salary, or remote status. It is typically the landing screen and leads into a Job Listing Detail screen. It is expected because job boards aggregate many postings that need filtering to be navigable; also called browse jobs, search jobs, or job listings." },
    { category: "job-board", title: "Job Listing Detail", content: "The job listing detail screen shows the full description, requirements, and compensation for a single role. It follows Job Search and leads into the Application Form. It is expected because candidates need enough information to decide whether a role fits before applying; also called job posting, job description, or role detail." },
    { category: "job-board", title: "Company Profile", content: "The company profile screen shows information about the hiring organization, including culture, size, and other open roles. It is typically reached from Job Listing Detail and can lead back into Job Search filtered by that company. It is expected because candidates often evaluate the employer, not just the role; also called employer profile or about the company." },
    { category: "job-board", title: "Application Form", content: "The application form screen collects the candidate's resume, cover letter, and answers to screening questions for a specific role. It follows Job Listing Detail and, on submission, leads to a confirmation and Application Tracking. It is expected as the core conversion action of any job board; also called apply now, submit application, or job application." },
    { category: "job-board", title: "Resume/Profile Upload", content: "This screen lets a candidate upload or build a resume and professional profile used across applications. It is typically completed once during account setup or the first Application Form, and is reused for subsequent applications. It is expected because resumes are the standard credential document in hiring; also called upload resume, build profile, or CV upload." },
    { category: "job-board", title: "Application Tracking", content: "The application tracking screen lists jobs a candidate has applied to along with their current status, such as submitted, under review, or interview. It is reached from account navigation after an Application Form is submitted. It is expected because candidates want visibility into where their applications stand; also called my applications, application status, or applied jobs." },
    { category: "job-board", title: "Saved Jobs", content: "The saved jobs screen lists roles a candidate has bookmarked from Job Search or Job Listing Detail to apply to later. It is reached from account navigation and leads back into Job Listing Detail. It is expected because job searching typically spans multiple sessions; also called favorites, bookmarked jobs, or watchlist." },
    { category: "job-board", title: "Interview Scheduling", content: "The interview scheduling screen lets a candidate pick an available time slot for an interview once selected by an employer. It follows a status update in Application Tracking and leads to a confirmed interview entry. It is expected because coordinating interview logistics is a common downstream step after initial screening; also called schedule interview or book a time." },

    { category: "content-publishing", title: "Homepage/Feed", content: "The homepage shows a curated or chronological feed of recent articles, often organized by section or editorial priority. It is the default landing screen and leads into an Article Detail screen when a headline is tapped. It is expected because publishers need a central entry point showcasing their latest and most important content; also called front page, homepage, or article feed." },
    { category: "content-publishing", title: "Article Detail", content: "The article detail screen displays the full text of a single piece of content, along with author byline, publish date, and related articles. It follows the Homepage/Feed or Search and leads into the Comment Section. It is expected as the core content-consumption screen of any publishing platform; also called article page, post detail, or story view." },
    { category: "content-publishing", title: "Author Profile", content: "The author profile screen shows an author's bio and a list of their other published articles. It is typically reached by tapping a byline on Article Detail. It is expected because readers often follow specific writers whose work they trust; also called writer profile, contributor page, or byline page." },
    { category: "content-publishing", title: "Comment Section", content: "The comment section shows reader responses to an article along with an input to add a new comment. It appears below or attached to Article Detail. It is expected because reader discussion and engagement are core to most publishing platforms; also called discussion, reader comments, or responses." },
    { category: "content-publishing", title: "Category/Tag Browse", content: "The category browse screen lists articles filtered by a specific topic, section, or tag. It is reached from the Homepage/Feed navigation or from a tag link on Article Detail, and leads back into Article Detail. It is expected because readers often want to explore a specific subject area rather than the general feed; also called section page, topic page, or tag archive." },
    { category: "content-publishing", title: "Search", content: "The search screen lets a user find articles by keyword, returning a filtered results list. It is reached from a search icon on the Homepage/Feed and leads into Article Detail. It is expected because large content archives need direct lookup beyond browsing; also called search results or find articles." },
    { category: "content-publishing", title: "Subscription/Paywall", content: "The subscription paywall screen appears when a reader hits a free-article limit or tries to access premium content, prompting them to subscribe. It typically interrupts Article Detail and leads into a Payment step. It is expected because many publishers monetize through metered or hard paywalls; also called subscribe now, paywall, or premium content prompt." },
    { category: "content-publishing", title: "Newsletter Signup", content: "The newsletter signup screen or widget collects a reader's email to receive periodic content digests. It commonly appears as a banner on the Homepage/Feed or at the end of an Article Detail page. It is expected because email remains a key retention channel for publishers; also called email signup, subscribe to newsletter, or get updates." },

    { category: "authentication", title: "Sign Up", content: "The sign up screen lets a new user create an account by providing credentials such as email and password, or via a social login option. It is typically the first screen for new users, preceding Email Verification or Onboarding. It is expected as the universal entry point for any app that persists user data; also called create account, register, or get started." },
    { category: "authentication", title: "Sign In", content: "The sign in screen lets a returning user authenticate with existing credentials to access their account. It is the entry point for returning users, leading into the app's main dashboard or feed, and links out to Password Reset. It is expected as the counterpart to Sign Up in any account-based product; also called log in, login, or authenticate." },
    { category: "authentication", title: "Password Reset", content: "The password reset screen lets a user who has forgotten their password request a reset link or code, usually sent via email or SMS. It is reached from a link on the Sign In screen and leads into a new-password entry step. It is expected because forgotten passwords are a routine occurrence in any credentialed system; also called forgot password, reset password, or recover account." },
    { category: "authentication", title: "Email Verification", content: "The email verification screen prompts a new user to confirm their email address, typically via a code or link, before gaining full account access. It follows Sign Up and precedes the main app experience or Onboarding. It is expected because verified contact information is needed for security and communication; also called verify email or confirm your email." },
    { category: "authentication", title: "Two-Factor Authentication", content: "The two-factor authentication screen asks a user to enter a one-time code from an app or SMS in addition to their password. It appears immediately after Sign In when 2FA is enabled, before granting access. It is expected in security-sensitive apps as an added layer beyond a single password; also called 2FA, OTP verification, or security code." },
    { category: "authentication", title: "Empty State", content: "The empty state screen appears when a newly authenticated user has no content or data yet, showing guidance toward their first action. It appears in place of the main dashboard or feed immediately after Sign Up or first Sign In. It is expected because every new account starts with nothing, and this screen prevents the app from feeling broken; also called zero state, no-data screen, or getting started prompt." }
]